@lang en

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `uk-interpretation-time`

-- The Interpretation Act 1978 (1978 c. 30), the sections and Schedule that say which of its provisions reach which enactment:
-- ss 22, 23, 25 and Schedule 2 (Parts I and II), with Schedule 1's year and date marks as Schedule 2 para 4 reads them.
--
-- Source: the converter-made plain-text rendering in ../../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1978-30.txt, not the official text (see SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
--
-- THE QUESTION THIS MODULE ANSWERS
--   Given a provision of the Act and an enactment (an Act, or subordinate legislation) with the day it was passed or made:
--   does the provision reach it, and in whole or in part?
--   The answer is an `Extent of application`. `does not apply` means that the text applies nothing there: s 22(1) applies the Act to
--   Acts passed before the commencement "to the extent specified in Part I of Schedule 2", and s 23(1) to earlier subordinate
--   legislation "to the extent specified in Part II of Schedule 2", so what those Parts do not list does not apply.
--
-- HOW AN ENACTMENT'S DAY IS READ
--   "after the commencement" is read as on or after 1 January 1979, since s 26 brings the Act into force at the beginning of that day
--   (fork F9). "after the year 1889" is read as a year greater than 1889: an Act passed on 31 December 1889 is not after the year 1889.
--   "passed" means the day of Royal Assent for an Act (s 4(b)) and "made" the day on which subordinate legislation is made.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO (coverage table in NOTES.md, rows ss 22(2), 22(3) partly, 23ZA, 23A, 23B, 23C, 24)
--   The taxonomy of devolved and assimilated legislation is not built: those sections turn on Acts that are not among the sources
--   (the Scotland Act 1998, the Government of Wales Act 2006, the Legislation (Wales) Act 2019, the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018)
--   and on dates ("IP completion day", "exit day") that the text refers to without giving.
--
-- Whether a provision applies "so far as applicable" (s 23(1)) to a particular instrument is a question about that instrument, and
-- not one this module can settle; the answer `applies without limit` means that the Act does not itself cut the provision down.

§ `Nouns of this module`

-- The provisions that Schedule 2 lists, and those that it does not, so that a test can show that an unlisted one does not reach an
-- earlier Act. Section 5 and Schedule 1 are treated apart (Schedule 2 para 4, below). Sections 22 to 27 are about application itself.
DECLARE `Provision of the Act` IS ONE OF
    `section 1`
    `section 2`
    `section 3`
    `section 4(a)`
    `section 4(b)`
    `section 6(a)`
    `section 6(b)`
    `section 6(c)`
    `section 7`
    `section 8`
    `section 9`
    `section 10`
    `section 11`
    `section 12`
    `section 13`
    `section 14`
    `section 14A`
    `section 15`
    `section 16(1)`
    `section 16(2)`
    `section 17(1)`
    `section 17(2)(a)`
    `section 17(2)(b)`
    `section 18`
    `section 19(1)`
    `section 19(2)`
    `section 20(1)`
    `section 20(2)`
    `section 21`

DECLARE `Extent of application` IS ONE OF
    `applies without limit`
    `applies only so far as the enactment relates to an offence punishable on indictment or on summary conviction`
    `applies only so far as it relates to subordinate legislation made after the year 1889`
    `applies only so far as it relates to rules, regulations or byelaws`
    `does not apply`

-- Schedule 1 marks some paragraphs with a year or a date, and some with neither (Sch 2 para 4(1)).
DECLARE `Mark at the end of a Schedule 1 paragraph` IS ONE OF
    `no year or date is specified`
    `a year is specified`  HAS `the year` IS A NUMBER
    `a date is specified`  HAS `the date` IS A DATE

-- The expressions Schedule 2 Part II names for subordinate legislation made before the commencement.
DECLARE `Expression that Schedule 2 Part II applies` IS ONE OF
    `England`
    `Local land charges register and appropriate local land charges register`
    `in relation to Scotland, expressions relating to the police`
    `United Kingdom`
    `Wales`
    `county court, in relation to England and Wales`
    `enactment, in so far as it relates to assimilated direct legislation`

DECLARE `A Church measure` IS ONE OF
    `a Measure of the General Synod of the Church of England`
    `a Measure of the Church Assembly` HAS `the day it was passed` IS A DATE

§ `Section 22(1): the Act applies to itself, and to Acts passed after the commencement`

-- A refusal is an expression at any type; each name is its message.

-- src:103 | (1) This Act applies to itself, to any Act passed after the commencement of this Act (subject , in the case of section 20(2A) to (6), to the provision made in section 20(2A) or (3) and, in the case of section 20A, to the provision made in that section) and, to the extent specified in Part I of Schedule 2, to Acts passed before the commencement of this Act.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 22(1) (src:103): "This Act applies to itself"
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 22(1) provides that the Interpretation Act 1978 applies to itself` MEANS TRUE

§ `Part I of Schedule 2: the extent to which a provision reaches an Act passed before the commencement`

-- src:395 | 2 The following apply to Acts passed after the year 1850:—
-- src:396 | Section 1
-- src:397 | Section 2
-- src:398 | Section 3
-- src:399 | Section 6(a) and (c) so far as not applicable to such Acts by virtue of paragraph 1
-- src:400 | Section 15
-- src:401 | Section 17(1).
-- src:402 | 3 The following apply to Acts passed after the year 1889:—
-- src:403 | Section 4
-- src:404 | Section 7
-- src:405 | Section 8
-- src:406 | Section 12
-- src:407 | Section 13
-- src:408 | Section 14 so far as it relates to rules, regulations or byelaws
-- src:409 | Section 16(1)
-- src:410 | Section 17(2)(a)
-- src:411 | Section 19(1)
-- src:412 | Section 20(1).
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 2 paras 2 and 3 (src:395-412): the whole of a provision, for an Act passed after a year
GIVEN year IS A NUMBER
      day  IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Extent of application`
`the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` year day MEANS
    IF   DATE_YEAR day GREATER THAN year
    THEN `applies without limit`
    ELSE `does not apply`

-- Schedule 2 para 1 applies to Acts whenever passed: s 6(a) and (c) so far as they relate to offences punishable on indictment or on
-- summary conviction, ss 9, 10 and 14A and 18 and 19(2) in whole, and s 11 so far as it relates to subordinate legislation made after
-- the year 1889. Para 2 applies, to Acts passed after the year 1850, ss 1, 2, 3, 15 and 17(1) and the rest of s 6(a) and (c).
-- Para 3 applies, to Acts passed after the year 1889, ss 4, 7, 8, 12, 13, 16(1), 17(2)(a), 19(1) and 20(1) and s 14 so far as it
-- relates to rules, regulations or byelaws. The provisions that are not in any of the three lists do not apply: s 6(b), 16(2),
-- 17(2)(b), 20(2) and 21.
-- src:387 | 1 The following provisions of this Act apply to Acts whenever passed:—
-- src:388 | Section 6(a) and (c) so far as applicable to enactments relating to offences punishable on indictment or on summary conviction
-- src:389 | Section 9
-- src:390 | Section 10
-- src:391 | Section 11 so far as it relates to subordinate legislation made after the year 1889
-- src:392 | Section 14A
-- src:393 | Section 18
-- src:394 | Section 19(2).
-- src:395 | 2 The following apply to Acts passed after the year 1850:—
-- src:396 | Section 1
-- src:397 | Section 2
-- src:398 | Section 3
-- src:399 | Section 6(a) and (c) so far as not applicable to such Acts by virtue of paragraph 1
-- src:400 | Section 15
-- src:401 | Section 17(1).
-- src:402 | 3 The following apply to Acts passed after the year 1889:—
-- src:403 | Section 4
-- src:404 | Section 7
-- src:405 | Section 8
-- src:406 | Section 12
-- src:407 | Section 13
-- src:408 | Section 14 so far as it relates to rules, regulations or byelaws
-- src:409 | Section 16(1)
-- src:410 | Section 17(2)(a)
-- src:411 | Section 19(1)
-- src:412 | Section 20(1).
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 2 Part I paras 1 to 3 (src:387-412); s 22(1) (src:103)
GIVEN provision IS A `Provision of the Act`
      day       IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Extent of application`
`Part I of Schedule 2 — the extent to which the provision applies to an Act passed before the commencement` provision day MEANS
    CONSIDER provision
    WHEN `section 1`        THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1850 day
    WHEN `section 2`        THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1850 day
    WHEN `section 3`        THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1850 day
    WHEN `section 4(a)`     THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1889 day
    WHEN `section 4(b)`     THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1889 day
    WHEN `section 6(a)`     THEN
        IF   DATE_YEAR day GREATER THAN 1850
        THEN `applies without limit`
        ELSE `applies only so far as the enactment relates to an offence punishable on indictment or on summary conviction`
    WHEN `section 6(b)`     THEN `does not apply`
    WHEN `section 6(c)`     THEN
        IF   DATE_YEAR day GREATER THAN 1850
        THEN `applies without limit`
        ELSE `applies only so far as the enactment relates to an offence punishable on indictment or on summary conviction`
    WHEN `section 7`        THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1889 day
    WHEN `section 8`        THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1889 day
    WHEN `section 9`        THEN `applies without limit`
    WHEN `section 10`       THEN `applies without limit`
    WHEN `section 11`       THEN `applies only so far as it relates to subordinate legislation made after the year 1889`
    WHEN `section 12`       THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1889 day
    WHEN `section 13`       THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1889 day
    WHEN `section 14`       THEN
        IF   DATE_YEAR day GREATER THAN 1889
        THEN `applies only so far as it relates to rules, regulations or byelaws`
        ELSE `does not apply`
    WHEN `section 14A`      THEN `applies without limit`
    WHEN `section 15`       THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1850 day
    WHEN `section 16(1)`    THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1889 day
    WHEN `section 16(2)`    THEN `does not apply`
    WHEN `section 17(1)`    THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1850 day
    WHEN `section 17(2)(a)` THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1889 day
    WHEN `section 17(2)(b)` THEN `does not apply`
    WHEN `section 18`       THEN `applies without limit`
    WHEN `section 19(1)`    THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1889 day
    WHEN `section 19(2)`    THEN `applies without limit`
    WHEN `section 20(1)`    THEN `the whole of the provision applies to an Act passed after the year` 1889 day
    WHEN `section 20(2)`    THEN `does not apply`
    WHEN `section 21`       THEN `does not apply`

-- src:103 | (1) This Act applies to itself, to any Act passed after the commencement of this Act (subject , in the case of section 20(2A) to (6), to the provision made in section 20(2A) or (3) and, in the case of section 20A, to the provision made in that section) and, to the extent specified in Part I of Schedule 2, to Acts passed before the commencement of this Act.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 22(1) (src:103)
GIVEN provision IS A `Provision of the Act`
      day       IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Extent of application`
`the extent to which the provision applies to an Act passed on` provision day MEANS
    IF   `the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` day
    THEN `applies without limit`
    ELSE `Part I of Schedule 2 — the extent to which the provision applies to an Act passed before the commencement` provision day

§ `Section 23(1) and Part II of Schedule 2: subordinate legislation`

-- "The provisions of this Act, except sections 1 to 3 and 4(b), apply, so far as applicable and unless the contrary intention appears,
-- to subordinate legislation made after the commencement of this Act".
-- src:107 | (1) The provisions of this Act, except sections 1 to 3 and 4(b), apply, so far as applicable and unless the contrary intention appears, to subordinate legislation made after the commencement of this Act and, to the extent specified in Part II of Schedule 2, to subordinate legislation made before the commencement of this Act, as they apply to Acts.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 23(1) (src:107)
GIVEN provision IS A `Provision of the Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 23(1) excepts the provision` provision MEANS
    CONSIDER provision
    WHEN `section 1`    THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 2`    THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 3`    THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 4(b)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- Part II para 6: ss 4(a), 9 and 19(1) apply to subordinate legislation made at any time before the commencement "as they apply to
-- Acts passed at that time". Nothing else in Part II is a section: the rest of Part II is about Schedule 1 (paras 6, 7 and 8, below).
-- src:428 | Part II — Subordinate Legislation
-- src:429 | 6 Sections 4(a), 9 and 19(1), and so much of Schedule 1 as defines the following expressions, namely—
-- src:436 | apply to subordinate legislation made at any time before the commencement of this Act as they apply to Acts passed at that time.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 2 Part II para 6 (src:428-429) and (src:436)
GIVEN provision IS A `Provision of the Act`
      day       IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Extent of application`
`Part II of Schedule 2 — the extent to which the provision applies to subordinate legislation made before the commencement` provision day MEANS
    CONSIDER provision
    WHEN `section 4(a)`  THEN `the extent to which the provision applies to an Act passed on` provision day
    WHEN `section 9`     THEN `the extent to which the provision applies to an Act passed on` provision day
    WHEN `section 19(1)` THEN `the extent to which the provision applies to an Act passed on` provision day
    OTHERWISE `does not apply`

-- src:107 | (1) The provisions of this Act, except sections 1 to 3 and 4(b), apply, so far as applicable and unless the contrary intention appears, to subordinate legislation made after the commencement of this Act and, to the extent specified in Part II of Schedule 2, to subordinate legislation made before the commencement of this Act, as they apply to Acts.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 23(1) (src:107)
GIVEN provision IS A `Provision of the Act`
GIVETH AN `Extent of application`
`section 23(1) — the extent to which the provision applies to subordinate legislation made after the commencement` provision MEANS
    IF   `section 23(1) excepts the provision` provision
    THEN `does not apply`
    ELSE `applies without limit`

-- src:107 | (1) The provisions of this Act, except sections 1 to 3 and 4(b), apply, so far as applicable and unless the contrary intention appears, to subordinate legislation made after the commencement of this Act and, to the extent specified in Part II of Schedule 2, to subordinate legislation made before the commencement of this Act, as they apply to Acts.
-- src:110 | (4) Subsections (1) and (2) of this section do not apply to Orders in Council made under section 5 of the Statutory Instruments Act 1946, section 1(3) of the Northern Ireland (Temporary Provisions) Act 1972 or Schedule 1 to the Northern Ireland Act 1974.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 23(1) (src:107); s 23(4) (src:110)
GIVEN provision IS A `Provision of the Act`
      day       IS A DATE
      `it is an Order in Council that section 23(4) names` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Extent of application`
`the extent to which the provision applies to subordinate legislation made on` provision day `it is an Order in Council that section 23(4) names` MEANS
    IF   `it is an Order in Council that section 23(4) names`
    THEN `does not apply`
    ELSE IF   `the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` day
         THEN `section 23(1) — the extent to which the provision applies to subordinate legislation made after the commencement` provision
         ELSE `Part II of Schedule 2 — the extent to which the provision applies to subordinate legislation made before the commencement` provision day

-- src:110 | (4) Subsections (1) and (2) of this section do not apply to Orders in Council made under section 5 of the Statutory Instruments Act 1946, section 1(3) of the Northern Ireland (Temporary Provisions) Act 1972 or Schedule 1 to the Northern Ireland Act 1974.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 23(4) (src:110): the Orders in Council that subsections (1) and (2) do not reach
GIVEN `it is an Order in Council made under section 5 of the Statutory Instruments Act 1946, section 1(3) of the Northern Ireland (Temporary Provisions) Act 1972 or Schedule 1 to the Northern Ireland Act 1974` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 23(4) provides that subsections (1) and (2) do not apply` `it is an Order in Council made under section 5 of the Statutory Instruments Act 1946, section 1(3) of the Northern Ireland (Temporary Provisions) Act 1972 or Schedule 1 to the Northern Ireland Act 1974` MEANS
    `it is an Order in Council made under section 5 of the Statutory Instruments Act 1946, section 1(3) of the Northern Ireland (Temporary Provisions) Act 1972 or Schedule 1 to the Northern Ireland Act 1974`

-- "Sections 9 and 19(1) also apply to deeds and other instruments and documents as they apply to Acts and subordinate legislation".
-- The deed is read as made at the time of the day given, so that the date bar of the provision is applied as for an Act passed then.
-- src:109 | (3) Sections 9 and 19(1) also apply to deeds and other instruments and documents as they apply to Acts and subordinate legislation; and in the application of section 17(2)(a) to Acts passed or subordinate legislation made after the commencement of this Act, the reference to any other enactment includes any deed or other instrument or document.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 23(3) (src:109)
GIVEN provision IS A `Provision of the Act`
      day       IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Extent of application`
`section 23(3) — the extent to which the provision applies to a deed or other instrument or document made on` provision day MEANS
    CONSIDER provision
    WHEN `section 9`     THEN `the extent to which the provision applies to an Act passed on` provision day
    WHEN `section 19(1)` THEN `the extent to which the provision applies to an Act passed on` provision day
    OTHERWISE `does not apply`

§ `Sections 22(3) and 25: Church measures, the Act of 1889, and the day that fixes the band`

-- "This Act applies to Measures of the General Synod of the Church of England (and, so far as it relates to Acts passed before the
-- commencement of this Act, to Measures of the Church Assembly passed after 28th May 1925) as it applies to Acts."
-- src:105 | (3) This Act applies to Measures of the General Synod of the Church of England (and, so far as it relates to Acts passed before the commencement of this Act, to Measures of the Church Assembly passed after 28th May 1925) as it applies to Acts.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 22(3) (src:105)
GIVEN measure IS A `A Church measure`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 22(3) applies the Act to the Church measure as it applies to Acts` measure MEANS
    CONSIDER measure
    WHEN `a Measure of the General Synod of the Church of England` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a Measure of the Church Assembly` d THEN d GREATER THAN (YMD 1925 5 28)

-- src:214 | (2) Without prejudice to section 17(2)(a), a reference to the Interpretation Act 1889, to any provision of that Act or to any other enactment repealed by this Act, whether occurring in another Act, in subordinate legislation, in Northern Ireland legislation or in any deed or other instrument or document, shall be construed as referring to this Act, or to the corresponding provision of this Act, as it applies to Acts passed at the time of the reference.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 25(2) (src:214)
GIVEN `the reference is to the Interpretation Act 1889, to a provision of it, or to another enactment repealed by the Interpretation Act 1978` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 25(2) provides that the reference is to be construed as referring to the Interpretation Act 1978` `the reference is to the Interpretation Act 1889, to a provision of it, or to another enactment repealed by the Interpretation Act 1978` MEANS
    `the reference is to the Interpretation Act 1889, to a provision of it, or to another enactment repealed by the Interpretation Act 1978`

-- "The provisions of this Act relating to Acts passed after any particular time do not affect the construction of Acts passed before
-- that time, though continued or amended by Acts passed thereafter." The band of an Act is fixed by the day it was passed, and not
-- by the day of an Act that continues or amends it.
-- src:215 | (3) The provisions of this Act relating to Acts passed after any particular time do not affect the construction of Acts passed before that time, though continued or amended by Acts passed thereafter.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 25(3) (src:215)
GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
GIVETH A DATE
`section 25(3) — the day that decides which provisions apply to the enactment` e MEANS
    e's `the day it was passed or made`

§ `Schedule 2 para 4: which Schedule 1 paragraphs reach an Act passed before the commencement`

-- src:413 | 4 (1) Subject to the following provisions of this paragraph—
-- src:414 | (a) paragraphs of Schedule 1 at the end of which a year or date . . . is specified or described apply, so far as applicable, to Acts passed on or after the date, or after the year, so specified or described: and
-- src:415 | (b) paragraphs of that Schedule at the end of which no year or date is specified or described apply, so far as applicable, to Acts passed at any time.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 2 para 4(1) (src:413-415)
GIVEN mark IS A `Mark at the end of a Schedule 1 paragraph`
      day  IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Schedule 2 para 4(1) — a Schedule 1 paragraph with the mark applies to an Act passed before the commencement, on` mark day MEANS
    CONSIDER mark
    WHEN `no year or date is specified` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a year is specified` y        THEN DATE_YEAR day GREATER THAN y
    WHEN `a date is specified` d        THEN day AT LEAST d

-- Acts passed after the commencement take the whole of Schedule 1 (s 22(1)), whatever the mark: Schedule 1's own note says the marks
-- "are relevant for the purposes of paragraph 4 of Schedule 2 (application to existing enactments)" (fork F14).
-- Subordinate legislation made after the commencement takes it as Acts do (s 23(1)); made before, only what paras 6 to 8 name.
-- src:103 | (1) This Act applies to itself, to any Act passed after the commencement of this Act (subject , in the case of section 20(2A) to (6), to the provision made in section 20(2A) or (3) and, in the case of section 20A, to the provision made in that section) and, to the extent specified in Part I of Schedule 2, to Acts passed before the commencement of this Act.
-- src:107 | (1) The provisions of this Act, except sections 1 to 3 and 4(b), apply, so far as applicable and unless the contrary intention appears, to subordinate legislation made after the commencement of this Act and, to the extent specified in Part II of Schedule 2, to subordinate legislation made before the commencement of this Act, as they apply to Acts.
-- src:413 | 4 (1) Subject to the following provisions of this paragraph—
-- src:414 | (a) paragraphs of Schedule 1 at the end of which a year or date . . . is specified or described apply, so far as applicable, to Acts passed on or after the date, or after the year, so specified or described: and
-- src:415 | (b) paragraphs of that Schedule at the end of which no year or date is specified or described apply, so far as applicable, to Acts passed at any time.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 22(1) (src:103); s 23(1) (src:107); Sch 2 para 4(1) (src:413-415)
GIVEN e    IS AN `An enactment`
      mark IS A `Mark at the end of a Schedule 1 paragraph`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a Schedule 1 paragraph with the mark applies to the enactment` e mark MEANS
    IF   `the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` (e's `the day it was passed or made`)
    THEN TRUE
    ELSE CONSIDER e's `its kind`
         WHEN `an Act` THEN `Schedule 2 para 4(1) — a Schedule 1 paragraph with the mark applies to an Act passed before the commencement, on` mark (e's `the day it was passed or made`)
         WHEN `subordinate legislation` THEN FALSE

-- Schedule 2 para 6 applies the named definitions to earlier subordinate legislation "as they apply to Acts passed at that time", so
-- each carries its own Schedule 1 mark: "England" [1st April 1974]; "United Kingdom" [12th April 1927]; the others carry none.
-- Para 7: the definition of "county court", in relation to England and Wales, applies to Orders in Council made after the year 1846.
-- Para 8: the definition of "enactment", in so far as it relates to assimilated direct legislation, applies as para 6 does.
-- src:429 | 6 Sections 4(a), 9 and 19(1), and so much of Schedule 1 as defines the following expressions, namely—
-- src:431 | England;
-- src:432 | Local land charges register and appropriate local land charges register;
-- src:433 | in relation to Scotland, expressions relating to the police;
-- src:434 | United Kingdom;
-- src:435 | Wales,
-- src:436 | apply to subordinate legislation made at any time before the commencement of this Act as they apply to Acts passed at that time.
-- src:437 | 7 The definition in Schedule 1 of “county court”, in relation to England and Wales, applies to Orders in Council made after the year 1846.
-- src:438 | 8 The definition in Schedule 1 of “enactment”, in so far as it relates to assimilated direct legislation, applies to subordinate legislation made at any time before the commencement of this Act as it applies to Acts passed at that time.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 2 Part II paras 6 to 8 (src:429-438)
GIVEN expression IS AN `Expression that Schedule 2 Part II applies`
      day        IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Schedule 2 Part II — the definition applies to subordinate legislation made before the commencement, on` expression day MEANS
    CONSIDER expression
    WHEN `England` THEN `Schedule 2 para 4(1) — a Schedule 1 paragraph with the mark applies to an Act passed before the commencement, on` (`a date is specified` (YMD 1974 4 1)) day
    WHEN `Local land charges register and appropriate local land charges register` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `in relation to Scotland, expressions relating to the police` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `United Kingdom` THEN `Schedule 2 para 4(1) — a Schedule 1 paragraph with the mark applies to an Act passed before the commencement, on` (`a date is specified` (YMD 1927 4 12)) day
    WHEN `Wales` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `county court, in relation to England and Wales` THEN DATE_YEAR day GREATER THAN 1846
    WHEN `enactment, in so far as it relates to assimilated direct legislation` THEN TRUE

-- src:421 | (4) The definition of “Lord Chancellor” does not apply to Acts passed before 1st October 1921 in which that expression was used in relation to Ireland only.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 Sch 2 para 4(4) (src:421)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      `the expression was used in relation to Ireland only` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Schedule 2 para 4(4) provides that the definition of Lord Chancellor does not apply to the Act` day `the expression was used in relation to Ireland only` MEANS
        day LESS THAN (YMD 1921 10 1)
    AND `the expression was used in relation to Ireland only`

§ `Section 5: the contrary intention`

-- src:17 | 5 Definitions.
-- src:18 | In any Act, unless the contrary intention appears, words and expressions listed in Schedule 1 to this Act are to be construed according to that Schedule.
@ref Interpretation Act 1978 s 5 (src:17-18)
GIVEN `the Schedule 1 paragraph applies to the enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 5 provides that the word is to be construed according to Schedule 1` `the Schedule 1 paragraph applies to the enactment` `the contrary intention appears` MEANS
        `the Schedule 1 paragraph applies to the enactment`
    AND NOT `the contrary intention appears`
